WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.200
Why does this matter?

2
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:04.496
A web application firewall
in prevention mode protects

3
00:00:04.496 --> 00:00:07.512
nothing if an attacker can
call the origin directly.

4
00:00:07.512 --> 00:00:10.104
The guide's dangers
table puts it this way:

5
00:00:10.104 --> 00:00:14.829
attackers bypass the firewall and the
DDoS controls by calling the origin,

6
00:00:14.829 --> 00:00:19.707
and the Front Door backend service tag
alone admits any customer's Front Door,

7
00:00:19.707 --> 00:00:23.975
as Learn's page, Secure traffic to
Azure Front Door origins, explains.

8
00:00:23.975 --> 00:00:26.488
The other direction matters just as much.

9
00:00:26.488 --> 00:00:30.316
A workload whose traffic leaves
Azure without inspection can

10
00:00:30.316 --> 00:00:33.850
reach a command-and-control
server, or send data out, unseen.

11
00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:37.683
So this module covers both:
origins only the edge can reach,

12
00:00:37.683 --> 00:00:41.037
with TLS one point two or
later and tested failover,

13
00:00:41.037 --> 00:00:45.429
and every workload subnet's default
route through Azure Firewall,

14
00:00:45.429 --> 00:00:49.730
with threat intelligence and
intrusion detection in deny mode.
